Here's a suggested outline of the interfaces you might consider for each layer:

**Layer 7: Application Layer (e.g., HTTP, FTP, SMTP)**
```cpp
class IApplicationLayer {
public:
    virtual void sendRequest(const std::string& request) = 0;
    virtual std::string receiveResponse() = 0;
};
```
**Layer 6: Presentation Layer (e.g., data formatting, compression)**
```cpp
class IPresentationLayer {
public:
    virtual std::string encode(const std::string& data) = 0;
    virtual std::string decode(const std::string& encodedData) = 0;
};
```
**Layer 5: Session Layer (e.g., connection establishment, termination)**
```cpp
class ISessionLayer {
public:
    virtual void establishConnection() = 0;
    virtual void terminateConnection() = 0;
    virtual bool isConnected() const = 0;
};
```
**Layer 4: Transport Layer (e.g., segmentation, reassembly)**
```cpp
class ITransportLayer {
public:
    virtual void segmentData(const std::string& data) = 0;
    virtual std::string reassembleData() = 0;
    virtual void checkSum(const std::string& data) = 0;
};
```
**Layer 3: Network Layer (e.g., routing, packet switching)**
```cpp
class INetworkLayer {
public:
    virtual void routePacket(const std::string& packet) = 0;
    virtual std::string receivePacket() = 0;
};
```
**Layer 2: Data Link Layer (e.g., framing, error detection)**
```cpp
class IDataLinkLayer {
public:
    virtual void frameData(const std::string& data) = 0;
    virtual std::string frameCheck(const std::string& framedData) = 0;
};
```
**Layer 1: Physical Layer (e.g., bit-level transmission)**
```cpp
class IPhysicalLayer {
public:
    virtual void transmitBit(const bool& bit) = 0;
    virtual bool receiveBit() = 0;
};
```
